window.AUTH=window.AUTH||{};window.AUTH["22-49"]={"d":"22-49","id":"2022/22-49","n":"Lora v. United States","briefs":[{"k":"petitioner","label":"Brief of petitioner Efrain Lora","date":"2023-01-20","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22-49/252867/20230120180222246_22-49%20Brief%20for%20Petitioner.pdf","side":"pet","n":62},{"k":"respondent","label":"Brief of respondent United States","date":"2023-02-21","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22-49/255223/20230221162953870_22-49%20Lora%20v.%20USA.pdf","side":"res","n":96},{"k":"reply","label":"Reply of petitioner Efrain Lora","date":"2023-03-17","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22-49/259301/20230317123302116_22-49%20merits%20rb.pdf","side":"pet","n":36}],"opinion":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/22pdf/599us1r41_2bo2.pdf","tr":true,"items":[{"t":"case","label":"Abbott v. United States","cite":"562 U.S. 8","y":2010,"id":"2010/09-479","sc":1,"by":{"0":"18, 31–32, 34","1":"20, 29, 32, 33","2":"6, 20–22"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"transferred the bar on concurrent sentences to § 924(c)(1)(D)(ii).","cite":"562 U.S. at 18"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"for substantive offenses codified in other provisions.","cite":"562 U.S. 8, 27"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"its insistence that sentencing judges impose additional punishment for § 924(c) violations.","cite":"562 U.S. at 20"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"transfer[ring] the bar on concurrent sentences to § 924(c)(1)(D)(ii),","cite":"562 U.S. at 18"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"the worst offenders would often secure the shortest sentences,","cite":"562 U.S. at 21"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Ross","text":"And we think that it's just completely implausible that at the time that Congress was enacting this statute, it meant to keep a mandatory minimum consecutive sentence, as this Court explained in Abbott, explained three -- the -- Congress explained three times over that it's so important that individuals who engage in that base offense conduct receive that mandatory minimum consecutive sentence but at the same time eliminated that when the person actually takes the gun out and kills someone."},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Ross","text":"I'd point to this Court's decision in Abbott, where it rejected implausible, bizarre results under 924(c). It -- it's quite implausible that Congress intended when you actually take the gun out and kill someone with it for a lesser sentence to be possible than when it's simply in -- in the --"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Ross","text":"So -- so I think that's just not the way this Court has thought about 924(c). If you look at Abbott, I think the Court could have said precisely that in Abbott. It was construing the \"in addition to\" language."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Alleyne v. United States","cite":"570 U.S. 99","y":2013,"id":"2012/11-9335","sc":1,"by":{"0":"22, 24, 28","1":"5, 28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Anderson v. Wilson","cite":"289 U.S. 20","y":1933,"id":"scdb/1932-059","sc":1,"by":{"0":"33"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"We do not pause to consider whether a statute differently conceived and framed would yield results more consonant with fairness and reason. We take the statute as we find it.","cite":"289 U.S. 20, 27"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Andrus v. Glover Constr. Co.","cite":"446 U.S. 608","y":1980,"id":"1979/79-48","sc":1,"by":{"0":"20","2":"7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Apprendi v. New Jersey","cite":"530 U.S. 466","y":2000,"id":"1999/99-478","sc":1,"by":{"0":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Argentina v. Weltover, Inc.","cite":"504 U.S. 607","y":1992,"id":"1991/91-763","sc":1,"by":{"0":"33","2":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Atl. Cleaners & Dyers, Inc. v. United States","cite":"286 U.S. 427","y":1932,"id":"scdb/1931-075","sc":1,"by":{"0":"15"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"there is a natural presumption that identical words used in different parts of the same act are intended to have the same meaning.","cite":"286 U.S. 427, 433"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Beckles v. United States","cite":"137 S. Ct. 886","y":2017,"id":"2016/15-8544","sc":1,"by":{"0":"37"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"[f]or generations, legislatures have relied on individual judicial judgment to balance casespecific equities in order to impose a fair sentence.","cite":"137 S. Ct. 886"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Bifulco v. United States","cite":"447 U.S. 381","y":1980,"id":"1979/79-5010","sc":1,"by":{"0":"30","1":"21"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"not only to interpretations of the substantive ambit of criminal prohibitions, but also to the penalties they impose.","cite":"447 U.S. 381, 387"},{"src":"argument","who":"Mr. Rosenberg","text":"In fact, in the Bifulco case that we cited in our brief, it -- the -- the Court specifically said that you -- you arise under a specific subsection or section when your conviction, trial, and sentence is based on that subsection."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Blockburger v. United States","cite":"284 U.S. 299","y":1932,"id":"scdb/1931-120","sc":1,"by":{"0":"30","1":"8, 22, 23","2":"15, 17"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"‘each statute requires proof of an additional fact which the other does not.’","cite":"284 U.S. 299, 304"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Ross","text":"And under the Court's Blockburger precedents, the most natural way to do that is to read Section 924(j) to create an aggravated version of the same Section 924(c) offense."},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Ross","text":"Your Honor, if you thought that -- that Congress just made a mistake, particularly, I -- you know, we haven't really discussed the Blockburger question, I do want to get there, but if you thought that Congress made a mistake such that, you know, the -- the right result from the language was that this is all one offense, we have to charge (j) or (c), but when we charge (j), when we recognize what Congress recognized is a far more serious offense, then we lose the -- the mandatory minimum consecutive penalty in (c), you know, I think that might be a case for an absurdity type of construction bec …"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Ross","text":"I think that doesn't make sense, and I think, if you come to a reading where you think these are separate penalty provisions, that's actually the best evidence that these are, in fact, separate offenses for Blockburger."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Brown v. Ohio","cite":"432 U.S. 161","y":1977,"id":"1976/75-6933","sc":1,"by":{"0":"28","1":"22, 23"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"[It] protects against a second prosecution for the same offense after acquittal. It protects against a second prosecution for the same offense after conviction. And it protects against multiple punishments for the same offense.","cite":"432 U.S. 161, 165"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Busic v. United States","cite":"446 U.S. 398","y":1980,"id":"1979/78-6020","sc":1,"by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Castillo v. United States","cite":"530 U.S. 120","y":2000,"id":"1999/99-658","sc":1,"by":{"0":"22–24","2":"15–16"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"in a single sentence, not broken up with dashes or separated into subsections.","cite":"530 U.S. at 125"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Dorszynski v. United States","cite":"418 U.S. 424","y":1974,"id":"1973/73-5284","sc":1,"by":{"0":"26","1":"37","2":"13"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"will not assume Congress to have intended such a departure from well-established doctrine without a clear expression to disavow it.","cite":"418 U.S. 424, 441"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., Inc.","cite":"545 U.S. 546","y":2005,"id":"2004/04-70","sc":1,"by":{"0":"35"}},{"t":"case","label":"Food Mktg. Inst. v. Argus Leader Media","cite":"139 S. Ct. 2356","y":2019,"id":"2018/18-481","sc":1,"by":{"0":"11, 24","2":"3"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"In statutory interpretation disputes, a court’s proper starting point lies in a careful examination of the ordinary meaning and structure of the law itself.","cite":"139 S. Ct. 2356, 2364"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Garrett v. United States","cite":"471 U.S. 773","y":1985,"id":"1984/83-1842","sc":1,"by":{"0":"29–30","1":"22, 23","2":"17–18"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"There is nothing in the Constitution which prevents Congress from punishing separately each step leading to the consummation of a transaction which it has power to prohibit and punishing also the completed transaction.","cite":"471 U.S. 773, 779"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"punish[ ] separately each step leading to the consummation of a transaction which it has power to prohibit and punish[ ] also the completed transaction.","cite":"471 U.S. 773, 779"},{"src":"argument","who":"Ms. Ross","text":"That is because, you know, (c) -- (j) cross-references (c), they're in the same section, they're part of the same -- you know, they're a greater and lesser included offense, cases like Brown versus Ohio and Garrett, which strongly suggest they're one offense."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Greenlaw v. United States","cite":"554 U.S. 237","y":2008,"id":"2007/07-330","sc":1,"by":{"0":"18","1":"20, 33","2":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hamling v. United States","cite":"418 U.S. 87","y":1974,"id":"1973/73-507","sc":1,"by":{"0":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"ICC v. Parker","cite":"326 U.S. 60","y":1945,"id":"scdb/1944-091","sc":1,"by":{"0":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Iselin v. United States","cite":"270 U.S. 245","y":1926,"id":"scdb/1925-076","sc":1,"by":{"0":"13"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"To supply omissions transcends the judicial function.","cite":"270 U.S. 245, 251"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Kimbrough v. United States","cite":"552 U.S. 85","y":2007,"id":"2007/06-6330","sc":1,"by":{"0":"26","1":"37","2":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"King v. Wilkes","cite":"19 How. St. Tr. 1075","y":1770,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/How.%20St.%20Tr./19/1075/","by":{"0":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Koon v. United States","cite":"518 U.S. 81","y":1996,"id":"1995/94-1664","sc":1,"by":{"0":"26"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"has been uniform and constant in the federal judicial tradition.","cite":"518 U.S. 81, 113"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Koons Buick Pontiac GMC, Inc. v. Nigh","cite":"543 U.S. 50","y":2004,"id":"2004/03-377","sc":1,"by":{"0":"14–15","2":"4"},"q":[{"src":"argument","who":"Mr. Rosenberg","text":"And that distinction is compelled by this Court's precedent in Koons Buick. Moreover, there is nothing in the penalty provision of subsection (j) that refers to subsection (c)."}]},{"t":"case","label":"Lockett v. Ohio","cite":"438 U.S. 586","y":1978,"id":"1977/76-6997","sc":1,"by":{"0":"26, 37"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"[T]he concept of individualized sentencing in criminal cases … has long been accepted in this country.","cite":"438 U.S. at 602"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Ohio v. Johnson","cite":"467 U.S. 493","y":1984,"id":"1983/83-904","sc":1,"by":{"0":"29–30","1":"22"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"In contrast to the double jeopardy protection against multiple trials, the final component of double jeopardy—protection against cumulative punishments—is designed to ensure that the sentencing discretion of courts is confined to the limits established by the legislature.","cite":"467 U.S. 493, 499"},{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"[b]ecause the substantive power to prescribe crimes and determine punishments is vested with the legislature, the question under the Double Jeopardy Clause whether punishments are ‘multiple’ is essentially one of legislative intent.","cite":"467 U.S. at 499"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"multiple punishments for the same offense.","cite":"467 U.S. 493, 498"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Oregon v. Ice","cite":"555 U.S. 160","y":2009,"id":"2008/07-901","sc":1,"by":{"0":"12, 25","1":"36"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"a judge’s imposition of consecutive, rather than concurrent, sentences was the prevailing practice,","cite":"555 U.S. at 169"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Robert C. Herd & Co. v. Krawill Mach. Corp.","cite":"359 U.S. 297","y":1959,"id":"1958/276","sc":1,"by":{"0":"25","2":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Rodriguez v. United States","cite":"480 U.S. 522","y":1987,"id":"1986/86-5504","sc":1,"by":{"0":"34","2":"24"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"Deciding what competing values will or will not be sacrificed to the achievement of a particular objective is the very essence of legislative choice—and it frustrates rather than effectuates legislative intent simplistically to assume that whatever furthers the statute’s primary objective must be the law.","cite":"480 U.S. 522, 525–26"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Setser v. United States","cite":"566 U.S. 231","y":2012,"id":"2011/10-7387","sc":1,"by":{"0":"26","1":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Shady Grove Orthopedic Assocs. v. Allstate Ins. Co.","cite":"559 U.S. 393","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-1008","sc":1,"by":{"0":"35"}},{"t":"case","label":"Simpson v. United States","cite":"435 U.S. 6","y":1978,"id":"1977/76-5796","sc":1,"by":{"0":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Stirone v. United States","cite":"361 U.S. 212","y":1960,"id":"1959/35","sc":1,"by":{"0":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"TRW Inc. v. Andrews","cite":"534 U.S. 19","y":2001,"id":"2001/00-1045","sc":1,"by":{"0":"16, 21–22","2":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"U.S. ex rel. Att’y Gen. v. Del. & Hudson Co.","cite":"213 U.S. 366","y":1909,"id":"scdb/1908-137","sc":1,"by":{"0":"27","2":"15"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"[W]here a statute is susceptible of two constructions, by one of which grave and doubtful constitutional questions arise and by the other of which such questions are avoided, [a court’s] duty is to adopt the latter.","cite":"213 U.S. 366, 408"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Barrett","cite":"937 F.3d 126","y":2019,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/937/126/","by":{"0":"6–7","1":"6, 7"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Bass","cite":"404 U.S. 336","y":1971,"id":"1971/70-71","sc":1,"by":{"0":"30"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"ambiguity concerning the ambit of criminal statutes should be resolved in favor of lenity.","cite":"404 U.S. 336, 347"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Berrios","cite":"676 F.3d 118","y":2012,"court":"3d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/676/118/","by":{"0":"33","1":"15, 18, 25, 31"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"It takes no special insight or leap of logic to conclude that the central reason for Congress’s choice of language in writing subsection ( j)—‘[in] the course of a violation of subsection (c)’—was to ensure that separating out subsection ( j) from subsection (c) did not deprive the law of a coherent sentencing scheme.","cite":"676 F.3d at 1"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"in cases where there are actual murder victims.","cite":"676 F.3d at 141"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Dinwiddie","cite":"618 F.3d 821","y":2010,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/618/821/","by":{"0":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Gonzales","cite":"520 U.S. 1","y":1997,"id":"1996/95-1605","sc":1,"by":{"0":"17, 31","1":"20, 21","2":"6"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"When Congress enacted § 924(c)’s consecutive-sentencing provision … it cabined the sentencing discretion of district courts in a single circumstance: When a defendant violates § 924(c), his sentencing enhancement under that statute must run consecutively to all other prison terms.","cite":"520 U.S. 1, 9–10"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"cabin[ing] the sentencing discretion of district courts in a single circumstance.","cite":"520 U.S. at 9"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"discretion under the Sentencing Guidelines * * * in cases where related offenses are prosecuted in multiple proceedings, to establish sentences with an eye toward having such punishments approximate the total penalty that would have been imposed had the sentences for the different offenses been imposed at the same time.","cite":"520 U.S. at 9"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Julian","cite":"633 F.3d 1250","y":2011,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/633/1250/","by":{"0":"12, 22–23, 29, 35","2":"3, 10, 14, 17, 19"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"potentially increases the sentence for a crime when the criminal uses a firearm.","cite":"633 F.3d at 1256"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Palmer","cite":"2021 WL 3932027","y":2021,"court":"S.D.N.Y. Sept. 1","by":{"0":"5"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"during and in relation to, and in furtherance of, a conspiracy to distribute cocaine, and … us[ing] the firearm to cause the death","cite":"2021 WL 3932027"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Smith","cite":"756 F.3d 1179","y":2014,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/756/1179/","by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Young","cite":"561 F. App’x 85","y":2014,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20App%E2%80%99x/561/85/","by":{"0":"7, 33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Williams v. People of State of N.Y.","cite":"337 U.S. 241","y":1949,"id":"scdb/1948-087","sc":1,"by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 924","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/924","subs":["(e)","(e)(2)(B)(i)","(c)","(c)(1)(1988)","(c)(1)","(c)(1)(A)","(c)(1)(A)(i)","(c)(1)(A)(ii)","(c)(1)(A)(iii)","(c)(1)(B)","(c)(1)(C)","(c)(1)(D)","(c)(1)(D)(ii)","(c)(2)","(c)(3)","(c)(5)","(c)(5)(A)","(c)(5)(B)","(g)(4)","(i)(1994)","(l)","(m)"],"stext":{"head":"§924. Penalties","paras":[[0,"(a)(1) Except as otherwise provided in this subsection, subsection (b), (c), (f), or (p) of this section, or in section 929, whoever—"],[1,"(A) knowingly makes any false statement or representation with respect to the information required by this chapter to be kept in the records of a person licensed under this chapter or in applying for any license or exemption or relief from disability under the provisions of this chapter;"],[1,"(B) knowingly violates subsection (a)(4), (f), (k), or (q) of section 922;"],[1,"(C) knowingly imports or brings into the United States or any possession thereof any firearm or ammunition in violation of section 922(l); or"],[1,"(D) willfully violates any other provision of this chapter,"],[0,"shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than five years, or both."],[0,"(2) Whoever knowingly violates subsection (a)(6), (h), (i), (j), or (o) of section 922 shall be fined as provided in this title, imprisoned not more than 10 years, or both."],[0,"(3) Any licensed dealer, licensed importer, licensed manufacturer, or licensed collector who knowingly—"],[1,"(A) makes any false statement or representation with respect to the information required by the provisions of this chapter to be kept in the records of a person licensed under this chapter, or"],[1,"(B) violates subsection (m) of section 922,"],[0,"shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than one year, or both."],[0,"(4) Whoever violates section 922(q) shall be fined under this title, imprisoned for not more than 5 years, or both. Notwithstanding any other provision of law, the term of imprisonment imposed under this paragraph shall not run concurrently with any other term of imprisonment imposed under any other provision of law. Except for the authorization of a term of imprisonment of not more than 5 years made in this paragraph, for the purpose of any other law a violation of section 922(q) shall be deemed to be a misdemeanor."],[0,"(5) Whoever knowingly violates subsection (s) or (t) of section 922 shall be fined under this title, imprisoned for not more than 1 year, or both."],[0,"(6)(A)(i) A juvenile who violates section 922(x) shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than 1 year, or both, except that a juvenile described in clause (ii) shall be sentenced to probation on appropriate conditions and shall not be incarcerated unless the juvenile fails to comply with a condition of probation."],[0,"(ii) A juvenile is described in this clause if—"],[1,"(I) the offense of which the juvenile is charged is possession of a handgun or ammunition in violation of section 922(x)(2); and"],[1,"(II) the juvenile has not been convicted in any court of an offense (including an offense under section 922(x) or a similar State law, but not including any other offense consisting of conduct that if engaged in by an adult would not constitute an offense) or adjudicated as a juvenile delinquent for conduct that if engaged in by an adult would constitute an offense."],[0,"(B) A person other than a juvenile who knowingly violates section 922(x)—"],[1,"(i) shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than 1 year, or both; and"],[1,"(ii) if the person sold, delivered, or otherwise transferred a handgun or ammunition to a juvenile knowing or having reasonable cause to know that the juvenile intended to carry or otherwise possess or discharge or otherwise use the handgun or ammunition in the commission of a crime of violence, shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than 10 years, or both."],[0,"(7) Whoever knowingly violates section 931 shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than 3 years, or both."],[0,"(8) Whoever knowingly violates subsection (d) or (g) of section 922 shall be fined under this title, imprisoned for not more than 15 years, or both."],[0,"(b) Whoever, with intent to commit therewith an offense punishable by imprisonment for a term exceeding one year, or with knowledge or reasonable cause to believe that an offense punishable by imprisonment for a term exceeding one year is to be committed therewith, ships, transports, or receives a firearm or any ammunition in interstate or foreign commerce shall be fined under this title, or imprisoned not more than ten years, or both."],[0,"(c)(1)(A) Except to the extent that a greater minimum sentence is otherwise provided by this subsection or by any other provision of law, any person who, during and in relation to any crime of violence or drug trafficking crime (including a crime of violence or drug trafficking crime that provides for an enhanced punishment if committed by the use of a deadly or dangerous weapon or device) for which the person may be prosecuted in a court of the United States, uses or carries a firearm, or who, in furtherance of any such crime, possesses a firearm, shall, in addition to the punishment provided for such crime of violence or drug trafficking crime—"],[1,"(i) be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 5 years;"],[1,"(ii) if the firearm is brandished, be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 7 years; and"],[1,"(iii) if the firearm is discharged, be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 10 years."],[0,"(B) If the firearm possessed by a person convicted of a violation of this subsection—"],[1,"(i) is a short-barreled rifle, short-barreled shotgun, or semiautomatic assault weapon, the person shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 10 years; or"],[1,"(ii) is a machinegun or a destructive device, or is equipped with a firearm silencer or firearm muffler, the person shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 30 years."],[0,"(C) In the case of a violation of this subsection that occurs after a prior conviction under this subsection has become final, the person shall—"],[1,"(i) be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 25 years; and"],[1,"(ii) if the firearm involved is a machinegun or a destructive device, or is equipped with a firearm silencer or firearm muffler, be sentenced to imprisonment for life."],[0,"(D) Notwithstanding any other provision of law—"],[1,"(i) a court shall not place on probation any person convicted of a violation of this subsection; and"],[1,"(ii) no term of imprisonment imposed on a person under this subsection shall run concurrently with any other term of imprisonment imposed on the person, including any term of imprisonment imposed for the crime of violence or drug trafficking crime during which the firearm was used, carried, or possessed."],[0,"(2) For purposes of this subsection, the term \"drug trafficking crime\" means any felony punishable under the Controlled Substances Act (21 U.S.C. 801 et seq.), the Controlled Substances Import and Export Act (21 U.S.C. 951 et seq.), or chapter 705 of title 46."],[0,"(3) For purposes of this subsection the term \"crime of violence\" means an offense that is a felony and—"],[1,"(A) has as an element the use, attempted use, or threatened use of physical force against the person or property of another, or"],[1,"(B) that by its nature, involves a substantial risk that physical force against the person or property of another may be used in the course of committing the offense."],[0,"(4) For purposes of this subsection, the term \"brandish\" means, with respect to a firearm, to display all or part of the firearm, or otherwise make the presence of the firearm known to another person, in order to intimidate that person, regardless of whether the firearm is directly visible to that person."],[0,"(5) Except to the extent that a greater minimum sentence is otherwise provided under this subsection, or by any other provision of law, any person who, during and in relation to any crime of violence or drug trafficking crime (including a crime of violence or drug trafficking crime that provides for an enhanced punishment if committed by the use of a deadly or dangerous weapon or device) for which the person may be prosecuted in a court of the United States, uses or carries armor piercing ammunition, or who, in furtherance of any such crime, possesses armor piercing ammunition, shall, in addition to the punishment provided for such crime of violence or drug trafficking crime or conviction under this section—"],[1,"(A) be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 15 years; and"],[1,"(B) if death results from the use of such ammunition—"],[2,"(i) if the killing is murder (as defined in section 1111), be punished by death or sentenced to a term of imprisonment for any term of years or for life; and"],[2,"(ii) if the killing is manslaughter (as defined in section 1112), be punished as provided in section 1112."],[0,"(d)(1) Any firearm or ammunition involved in or used in any knowing violation of subsection (a)(4), (a)(6), (f), (g), (h), (i), (j), or (k) of section 922, or knowing importation or bringing into the United States or any possession thereof any firearm or ammunition in violation of section 922(l), or knowing violation of section 924, 932, or 933, or willful violation of any other provision of this chapter or any rule or regulation promulgated thereunder, or any violation of any other criminal law of the United States, or any firearm or ammunition intended to be used in any offense referred to in paragraph (3) of this subsection, where such intent is demonstrated by clear and convincing evidence, shall be subject to seizure and forfeiture, and all provisions of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 relating to the seizure, forfeiture, and disposition of firearms, as defined in section 5845(a) of that Code, shall, so far as applicable, extend to seizures and forfeitures under the provisions of this chapter: Provided, That upon acquittal of the owner or possessor, or dismissal of the charges against him other than upon motion of the Government prior to trial, or lapse of or court termination of the restraining order to which he is subject, the seized or relinquished firearms or ammunition shall be returned forthwith to the owner or possessor or to a person delegated by the owner or possessor unless the return of the firearms or ammunition would place the owner or possessor or his delegate in violation of law. Any action or proceeding for the forfeiture of firearms or ammunition shall be commenced within one hundred and twenty days of such seizure."]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/18/924?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"by":{"0":"1–25, 27–38","1":"19, 33, 8, 9, 19, 5-32, 34, 35, 38-40, 30, 31, 31, 31, 31, 31, 31, 31, 31, 19, 19, 19, 19, 19, 19, 19, 30, 30, 19, 19, 19, 19, 19","2":"1–10, 12, 14–16, 18–22, 24"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"in the course of a violation of subsection (c).","cite":"18 U.S.C. § 924(j)"},{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"no term of imprisonment imposed on a person under this subsection shall run concurrently with any other term of imprisonment imposed on the person.","cite":"18 U.S.C. § 924(c)(1)(D)(ii)"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"imprisonment for any term of years or for life","cite":"18 U.S.C. 924"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"imposed * * * under this subsection","cite":"18 U.S.C. 924(c)(1)(D)(ii)"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"[N]o term of imprisonment imposed on a person under this subsection shall run concurrently with any other term of imprisonment.","cite":"18 U.S.C. § 924(c)(1)(D)(ii)"}]},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 1028A","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1028A","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 1791","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1791","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 2332b","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/2332b","by":{"0":"16","2":"16"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3146","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3146","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3584","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3584","subs":["(a)"],"stext":{"head":"§3584. Multiple sentences of imprisonment","paras":[[0,"(a) Imposition of Concurrent or Consecutive Terms.—If multiple terms of imprisonment are imposed on a defendant at the same time, or if a term of imprisonment is imposed on a defendant who is already subject to an undischarged term of imprisonment, the terms may run concurrently or consecutively, except that the terms may not run consecutively for an attempt and for another offense that was the sole objective of the attempt. Multiple terms of imprisonment imposed at the same time run concurrently unless the court orders or the statute mandates that the terms are to run consecutively. Multiple terms of imprisonment imposed at different times run consecutively unless the court orders that the terms are to run concurrently."],[0,"(b) Factors To Be Considered in Imposing Concurrent or Consecutive Terms.—The court, in determining whether the terms imposed are to be ordered to run concurrently or consecutively, shall consider, as to each offense for which a term of imprisonment is being imposed, the factors set forth in section 3553(a)."],[0,"(c) Treatment of Multiple Sentence as an Aggregate.—Multiple terms of imprisonment ordered to run consecutively or concurrently shall be treated for administrative purposes as a single, aggregate term of imprisonment."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/18/3584?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"by":{"0":"1, 4, 7, 11–13, 25, 35","1":"36, 10, 36","2":"11–13, 21"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":0,"text":"unless the court orders or the statute mandates that the terms are to run consecutively.","cite":"18 U.S.C. § 3584(a)"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"the statute mandates that the terms are to run consecutively.","cite":"18 U.S.C. 3584(a)"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"mandate[] that * * * terms are to run consecutively.","cite":"18 U.S.C. 3584(a)"}]},{"t":"usc","label":"21 U.S.C. § 841","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/21/841","subs":["(a)(1)","(b)(1)(A)(2000)","(b)(1)(A)(2012)"],"stext":{"head":"§841. Prohibited acts A","paras":[[0,"(a) Unlawful acts"],[0,"Except as authorized by this subchapter, it shall be unlawful for any person knowingly or intentionally—"],[1,"(1) to manufacture, distribute, or dispense, or possess with intent to manufacture, distribute, or dispense, a controlled substance; or"],[1,"(2) to create, distribute, or dispense, or possess with intent to distribute or dispense, a counterfeit substance."],[0,"(b) Penalties"],[0,"Except as otherwise provided in section 849, 859, 860, or 861 of this title, any person who violates subsection (a) of this section shall be sentenced as follows:"],[0,"(1)(A) In the case of a violation of subsection (a) of this section involving—"],[1,"(i) 1 kilogram or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of heroin;"],[1,"(ii) 5 kilograms or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of—"],[2,"(I) coca leaves, except coca leaves and extracts of coca leaves from which cocaine, ecgonine, and derivatives of ecgonine or their salts have been removed;"],[2,"(II) cocaine, its salts, optical and geometric isomers, and salts of isomers;"],[2,"(III) ecgonine, its derivatives, their salts, isomers, and salts of isomers; or"],[2,"(IV) any compound, mixture, or preparation which contains any quantity of any of the substances referred to in subclauses (I) through (III);"],[1,"(iii) 280 grams or more of a mixture or substance described in clause (ii) which contains cocaine base;"],[1,"(iv) 100 grams or more of phencyclidine (PCP) or 1 kilogram or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of phencyclidine (PCP);"],[1,"(v) 10 grams or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of lysergic acid diethylamide (LSD);"],[1,"(vi) 400 grams or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of N-phenyl-N- [ 1- ( 2-phenylethyl ) -4-piperidinyl ] propanamide or 100 grams or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of any analogue of N-phenyl-N-[1-(2-phenylethyl)-4-piperidinyl] propanamide;"],[1,"(vii) 1000 kilograms or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of marihuana, or 1,000 or more marihuana plants regardless of weight; or"],[1,"(viii) 50 grams or more of methamphetamine, its salts, isomers, and salts of its isomers or 500 grams or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of methamphetamine, its salts, isomers, or salts of its isomers;"],[0,"such person shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment which may not be less than 10 years or more than life and if death or serious bodily injury results from the use of such substance shall be not less than 20 years or more than life, a fine not to exceed the greater of that authorized in accordance with the provisions of title 18 or $10,000,000 if the defendant is an individual or $50,000,000 if the defendant is other than an individual, or both. If any person commits such a violation after a prior conviction for a serious drug felony or serious violent felony has become final, such person shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 15 years and not more than life imprisonment and if death or serious bodily injury results from the use of such substance shall be sentenced to life imprisonment, a fine not to exceed the greater of twice that authorized in accordance with the provisions of title 18 or $20,000,000 if the defendant is an individual or $75,000,000 if the defendant is other than an individual, or both. If any person commits a violation of this subparagraph or of section 849, 859, 860, or 861 of this title after 2 or more prior convictions for a serious drug felony or serious violent felony have become final, such person shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 25 years and fined in accordance with the preceding sentence. Notwithstanding section 3583 of title 18, any sentence under this subparagraph shall, in the absence of such a prior conviction, impose a term of supervised release of at least 5 years in addition to such term of imprisonment and shall, if there was such a prior conviction, impose a term of supervised release of at least 10 years in addition to such term of imprisonment. Notwithstanding any other provision of law, the court shall not place on probation or suspend the sentence of any person sentenced under this subparagraph. No person sentenced under this subparagraph shall be eligible for parole during the term of imprisonment imposed therein."],[0,"(B) In the case of a violation of subsection (a) of this section involving—"],[1,"(i) 100 grams or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of heroin;"],[1,"(ii) 500 grams or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of—"],[2,"(I) coca leaves, except coca leaves and extracts of coca leaves from which cocaine, ecgonine, and derivatives of ecgonine or their salts have been removed;"],[2,"(II) cocaine, its salts, optical and geometric isomers, and salts of isomers;"],[2,"(III) ecgonine, its derivatives, their salts, isomers, and salts of isomers; or"],[2,"(IV) any compound, mixture, or preparation which contains any quantity of any of the substances referred to in subclauses (I) through (III);"],[1,"(iii) 28 grams or more of a mixture or substance described in clause (ii) which contains cocaine base;"],[1,"(iv) 10 grams or more of phencyclidine (PCP) or 100 grams or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of phencyclidine (PCP);"],[1,"(v) 1 gram or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of lysergic acid diethylamide (LSD);"],[1,"(vi) 40 grams or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of N-phenyl-N- [ 1- ( 2-phenylethyl ) -4-piperidinyl ] propanamide or 10 grams or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of any analogue of N-phenyl-N-[1-(2-phenylethyl)-4-piperidinyl] propanamide;"],[1,"(vii) 100 kilograms or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of marihuana, or 100 or more marihuana plants regardless of weight; or"],[1,"(viii) 5 grams or more of methamphetamine, its salts, isomers, and salts of its isomers or 50 grams or more of a mixture or substance containing a detectable amount of methamphetamine, its salts, isomers, or salts of its isomers;"],[0,"such person shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment which may not be less than 5 years and not more than 40 years and if death or serious bodily injury results from the use of such substance shall be not less than 20 years or more than life, a fine not to exceed the greater of that authorized in accordance with the provisions of title 18 or $5,000,000 if the defendant is an individual or $25,000,000 if the defendant is other than an individual, or both. If any person commits such a violation after a prior conviction for a serious drug felony or serious violent felony has become final, such person shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment which may not be less than 10 years and not more than life imprisonment and if death or serious bodily injury results from the use of such substance shall be sentenced to life imprisonment, a fine not to exceed the greater of twice that authorized in accordance with the provisions of title 18 or $8,000,000 if the defendant is an individual or $50,000,000 if the defendant is other than an individual, or both. Notwithstanding section 3583 of title 18, any sentence imposed under this subparagraph shall, in the absence of such a prior conviction, include a term of supervised release of at least 4 years in addition to such term of imprisonment and shall, if there was such a prior conviction, include a term of supervised release of at least 8 years in addition to such term of imprisonment. Notwithstanding any other provision of law, the court shall not place on probation or suspend the sentence of any person sentenced under this subparagraph. No person sentenced under this subparagraph shall be eligible for parole during the term of imprisonment imposed therein."],[0,"(C) In the case of a controlled substance in schedule I or II, gamma hydroxybutyric acid (including when scheduled as an approved drug product for purposes of section 3(a)(1)(B) of the Hillory J. Farias and Samantha Reid Date-Rape Drug Prohibition Act of 2000), or 1 gram of flunitrazepam, except as provided in subparagraphs (A), (B), and (D), such person shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not more than 20 years and if death or serious bodily injury results from the use of such substance shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than twenty years or more than life, a fine not to exceed the greater of that authorized in accordance with the provisions of title 18 or $1,000,000 if the defendant is an individual or $5,000,000 if the defendant is other than an individual, or both. If any person commits such a violation after a prior conviction for a felony drug offense has become final, such person shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not more than 30 years and if death or serious bodily injury results from the use of such substance shall be sentenced to life imprisonment, a fine not to exceed the greater of twice that authorized in accordance with the provisions of title 18 or $2,000,000 if the defendant is an individual or $10,000,000 if the defendant is other than an individual, or both. Notwithstanding section 3583 of title 18, any sentence imposing a term of imprisonment under this paragraph shall, in the absence of such a prior conviction, impose a term of supervised release of at least 3 years in addition to such term of imprisonment and shall, if there was such a prior conviction, impose a term of supervised release of at least 6 years in addition to such term of imprisonment. Notwithstanding any other provision of law, the court shall not place on probation or suspend the sentence of any person sentenced under the provisions of this subparagraph which provide for a mandatory term of imprisonment if death or serious bodily injury results, nor shall a person so sentenced be eligible for parole during the term of such a sentence."]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/21/841?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"by":{"0":"5, 37","1":"5, 4, 5, 4","2":"21–22"}},{"t":"usc","label":"21 U.S.C. § 846","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/21/846","stext":{"head":"§846. Attempt and conspiracy","paras":[[0,"Any person who attempts or conspires to commit any offense defined in this subchapter shall be subject to the same penalties as those prescribed for the offense, the commission of which was the object of the attempt or conspiracy."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/21/846?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"by":{"0":"5","1":"3, 4","2":"21"}},{"t":"usc","label":"21 U.S.C. § 848","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/21/848","subs":["(e)(1)(A)"],"stext":{"head":"§848. Continuing criminal enterprise","paras":[[0,"(a) Penalties; forfeitures"],[0,"Any person who engages in a continuing criminal enterprise shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment which may not be less than 20 years and which may be up to life imprisonment, to a fine not to exceed the greater of that authorized in accordance with the provisions of title 18 or $2,000,000 if the defendant is an individual or $5,000,000 if the defendant is other than an individual, and to the forfeiture prescribed in section 853 of this title; except that if any person engages in such activity after one or more prior convictions of him under this section have become final, he shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment which may not be less than 30 years and which may be up to life imprisonment, to a fine not to exceed the greater of twice the amount authorized in accordance with the provisions of title 18 or $4,000,000 if the defendant is an individual or $10,000,000 if the defendant is other than an individual, and to the forfeiture prescribed in section 853 of this title."],[0,"(b) Life imprisonment for engaging in continuing criminal enterprise"],[0,"Any person who engages in a continuing criminal enterprise shall be imprisoned for life and fined in accordance with subsection (a), if—"],[1,"(1) such person is the principal administrator, organizer, or leader of the enterprise or is one of several such principal administrators, organizers, or leaders; and"],[1,"(2)(A) the violation referred to in subsection (c)(1) involved at least 300 times the quantity of a substance described in subsection 841(b)(1)(B) of this title, or"],[1,"(B) the enterprise, or any other enterprise in which the defendant was the principal or one of several principal administrators, organizers, or leaders, received $10 million dollars in gross receipts during any twelve-month period of its existence for the manufacture, importation, or distribution of a substance described in section 841(b)(1)(B) of this title."],[0,"(c) \"Continuing criminal enterprise\" defined"],[0,"For purposes of subsection (a), a person is engaged in a continuing criminal enterprise if—"],[1,"(1) he violates any provision of this subchapter or subchapter II the punishment for which is a felony, and"],[1,"(2) such violation is a part of a continuing series of violations of this subchapter or subchapter II—"],[2,"(A) which are undertaken by such person in concert with five or more other persons with respect to whom such person occupies a position of organizer, a supervisory position, or any other position of management, and"],[2,"(B) from which such person obtains substantial income or resources."],[0,"(d) Suspension of sentence and probation prohibited"],[0,"In the case of any sentence imposed under this section, imposition or execution of such sentence shall not be suspended, probation shall not be granted, and the Act of July 15, 1932 (D.C. Code, secs. 24–203—24–207), shall not apply."],[0,"(e) Death penalty"],[0,"(1) In addition to the other penalties set forth in this section—"],[1,"(A) any person engaging in or working in furtherance of a continuing criminal enterprise, or any person engaging in an offense punishable under section 841(b)(1)(A) 1 of this title or section 960(b)(1) 1 of this title who intentionally kills or counsels, commands, induces, procures, or causes the intentional killing of an individual and such killing results, shall be sentenced to any term of imprisonment, which shall not be less than 20 years, and which may be up to life imprisonment, or may be sentenced to death; and"],[1,"(B) any person, during the commission of, in furtherance of, or while attempting to avoid apprehension, prosecution or service of a prison sentence for, a felony violation of this subchapter or subchapter II who intentionally kills or counsels, commands, induces, procures, or causes the intentional killing of any Federal, State, or local law enforcement officer engaged in, or on account of, the performance of such officer's official duties and such killing results, shall be sentenced to any term of imprisonment, which shall not be less than 20 years, and which may be up to life imprisonment, or may be sentenced to death."],[0,"(2) As used in paragraph (1)(B), the term \"law enforcement officer\" means a public servant authorized by law or by a Government agency or Congress to conduct or engage in the prevention, investigation, prosecution or adjudication of an offense, and includes those engaged in corrections, probation, or parole functions."],[0,"(g) 2 to (p) Repealed. Pub. L. 109–177, title II, §221(2), Mar. 9, 2006, 120 Stat. 231"],[0,"(q) Repealed. Pub. L. 109–177, title II, §§221(4), 222(c), Mar. 9, 2006, 120 Stat. 231, 232"],[0,"(r) Repealed. Pub. L. 109–177, title II, §221(3), Mar. 9, 2006, 120 Stat. 231"],[0,"(s) Special provision for methamphetamine"],[0,"For the purposes of subsection (b), in the case of continuing criminal enterprise involving methamphetamine or its salts, isomers, or salts of isomers, paragraph (2)(A) shall be applied by substituting \"200\" for \"300\", and paragraph (2)(B) shall be applied by substituting \"$5,000,000\" for \"$10 million dollars\"."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/21/848?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"by":{"0":"5–6","1":"4, 5","2":"17–18"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1254","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1254","by":{"0":"4"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 90-618","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/82/1213","by":{"0":"31","1":"29"}},{"t":"law","label":"Protection of Lawful Commerce in Arms Act, Pub. L. No. 109-92","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/plaw/109/public/92","by":{"0":"36","1":"38"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 91-644","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/84/1880","by":{"0":"31","1":"29"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 105-386","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/plaw/105/public/386","by":{"0":"32","1":"31"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 98-473","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/98/1837","by":{"0":"12, 32"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 103-322","by":{"0":"32, 35","1":"30","2":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Barber v. Thomas","cite":"560 U.S. 474","y":2010,"id":"2009/09-5201","sc":1,"by":{"1":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Beecham v. United States","cite":"511 U.S. 368","y":1994,"id":"1993/93-445","sc":1,"by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Connecticut Nat’l Bank v. Germain","cite":"503 U.S. 249","y":1992,"id":"1991/90-1791","sc":1,"by":{"1":"39"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"would not render one or the other wholly superfluous,","cite":"503 U.S. 249, 253"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Crandon v. United States","cite":"494 U.S. 152","y":1990,"id":"1989/88-931","sc":1,"by":{"1":"40"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"that legislatures, not courts, define criminal liability,","cite":"494 U.S. 152, 158"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Dean v. United States","cite":"137 S. Ct. 1170","y":2017,"id":"2016/15-9260","sc":1,"by":{"1":"38"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kucana v. Holder","cite":"558 U.S. 233","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-911","sc":1,"by":{"1":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lamie v. United States Trustee","cite":"540 U.S. 526","y":2004,"id":"2003/02-693","sc":1,"by":{"1":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Loughrin v. United States","cite":"573 U.S. 351","y":2014,"id":"2013/13-316","sc":1,"by":{"1":"39"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"is not uncommon in criminal statutes,","cite":"573 U.S. 351, 358 n.4"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Luna Torres v. Lynch","cite":"578 U.S. 452","y":2016,"id":"2015/14-1096","sc":1,"by":{"1":"19"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"ha[s] routinely construed statutes to have a particular meaning even as [it] acknowledged that Congress could have expressed itself more clearly.","cite":"578 U.S. 452, 472"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Missouri v. Hunter","cite":"459 U.S. 359","y":1983,"id":"1982/81-1214","sc":1,"by":{"1":"22, 23"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"prevent[s] the sentencing court from prescribing greater punishment than the legislature intended.","cite":"459 U.S. 359, 366"},{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"specifically authorize[] cumulative punishment under two statutes, regardless of whether those two statutes proscribe the ‘same’ [offense] under Blockburger.","cite":"459 U.S. at 368"}]},{"t":"case","label":"National Ass’n of Mfrs. v. Department of Def.","cite":"138 S. Ct. 617","y":2018,"id":"2017/16-299","sc":1,"by":{"1":"13","2":"4–5, 8"}},{"t":"case","label":"New Process Steel, L. P. v. NLRB","cite":"560 U.S. 674","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-1457","sc":1,"by":{"1":"17"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"harmonize and give meaningful effect to all of the provisions.","cite":"560 U.S. 674, 680"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Russello v. United States","cite":"464 U.S. 16","y":1983,"id":"1983/82-472","sc":1,"by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"St. Louis Fuel & Supply Co. v. FERC","cite":"890 F.2d 446","y":1989,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/890/446/","by":{"1":"13","2":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Shular v. United States","cite":"140 S. Ct. 779","y":2020,"id":"2019/18-6662","sc":1,"by":{"1":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Simmons v. Himmelreich","cite":"578 U.S. 621","y":2016,"id":"2015/15-109","sc":1,"by":{"1":"18"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"presume[s] Congress says what it means and means what it says.","cite":"578 U.S. 621, 627"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Smith v. United States","cite":"508 U.S. 223","y":1993,"id":"1992/91-8674","sc":1,"by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Stokeling v. United States","cite":"139 S. Ct. 544","y":2019,"id":"2018/17-5554","sc":1,"by":{"1":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"United Savings Ass’n v. Timbers of Inwood Forest Assocs.","cite":"484 U.S. 365","y":1988,"id":"1987/86-1602","sc":1,"by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Allen","cite":"247 F.3d 741","y":2001,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/247/741/","by":{"1":"17"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"incorporates § 924(c) by reference without disclaiming the cumulative punishment scheme which is so clearly set out in § 924(c).","cite":"247 F.3d 741, 769"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Battle","cite":"289 F.3d 661","y":2002,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/289/661/","by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Bran","cite":"776 F.3d 276","y":2015,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/776/276/","by":{"1":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Castleman","cite":"572 U.S. 157","y":2014,"id":"2013/12-1371","sc":1,"by":{"1":"40"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"But ‘the rule of lenity only applies if, after considering text, structure, history, and purpose, there remains a grievous ambiguity or uncertainty in the statute, such that the Court must simply guess as to what Congress intended.’","cite":"572 U.S. 157, 172-173"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Curtis","cite":"324 F.3d 501","y":2003,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/324/501/","by":{"1":"24"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"that a defendant could be convicted and sentenced under (c) and ( j) at the same time.","cite":"324 F.3d 501, 507-509"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Dorsey","cite":"677 F.3d 944","y":2012,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/677/944/","by":{"1":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Gonzales","cite":"841 F.3d 339","y":2016,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/841/339/","by":{"1":"24-26"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"[S]ection 924( j) amounts to the ‘same offense’ as [S]ection 924(c) for purposes of the Double Jeopardy Clause.","cite":"841 F.3d 339, 356"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Jiménez-Torres","cite":"435 F.3d 3","y":2006,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/435/3/","by":{"1":"25"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"with the lesser-included offense of using a firearm during a federal crime of violence [or drug trafficking crime] in violation [of ] 18 U.S.C. § 924(c)(1)(A).","cite":"435 F.3d 3, 10"}]},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Palacios","cite":"982 F.3d 920","y":2020,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/982/920/","by":{"1":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Powell","cite":"469 U.S. 57","y":1984,"id":"1984/83-1307","sc":1,"by":{"1":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Texas","cite":"507 U.S. 529","y":1993,"id":"1992/91-1729","sc":1,"by":{"1":"36"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Young","cite":"561 Fed. Appx. 85","y":2014,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Fed.%20Appx./561/85/","by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment VI","clauses":[["sixth-trial","Speedy and Public Trial by Jury"],["confrontation","Confrontation Clause"],["counsel","Right to Counsel"]],"text":"In all criminal prosecutions, the accused shall enjoy the right to a speedy and public trial, by an impartial jury of the State and district wherein the crime shall have been committed, which district shall have been previously ascertained by law, and to be informed of the nature and cause of the accusation; to be confronted with the witnesses against him; to have compulsory process for obtaining witnesses in his favor, and to have the Assistance of Counsel for his defence.","by":{"1":"28"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 100-690","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/102/4373","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 101-647","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/104/4829","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 104-294","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/plaw/104/public/294","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"law","label":"Firearms Owners’ Protection Act, Pub. L. No. 99-308","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/100/456","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 2","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/2","by":{"1":"3, 4"},"q":[{"src":"argument","who":"Justice Alito","text":"-- if it's charged that way? I mean, it's very common for somebody to be -- to be charged with an offense in violation of 18 U.S.C. Section 2 and another provision."}]},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 1111","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1111","subs":["(b)"],"by":{"1":"14"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":1,"text":"[w]ithin the special maritime and territorial jurisdiction of the United States.","cite":"18 U.S.C. 1111(b)"}]},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 1112","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1112","subs":["(b)"],"stext":{"head":"§1112. Manslaughter","paras":[[0,"(a) Manslaughter is the unlawful killing of a human being without malice. It is of two kinds:"],[0,"Voluntary—Upon a sudden quarrel or heat of passion."],[0,"Involuntary—In the commission of an unlawful act not amounting to a felony, or in the commission in an unlawful manner, or without due caution and circumspection, of a lawful act which might produce death."],[0,"(b) Within the special maritime and territorial jurisdiction of the United States,"],[0,"Whoever is guilty of voluntary manslaughter, shall be fined under this title or imprisoned not more than 15 years, or both;"],[0,"Whoever is guilty of involuntary manslaughter, shall be fined under this title or imprisoned not more than 8 years, or both."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/18/1112?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"by":{"1":"15, 30, 15","2":"8–9, 23"},"q":[{"src":"argument","who":"Justice Alito","text":"Suppose a person is charged under (j)(2), a person who in the course of a violation of subsection (c) causes the death of a person through the use of a firearm, shall, if the killing is manslaughter, as defined in Section 1112, be punished as provided in that section."}]},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. Crim. P. 31","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcrmp/rule_31","subs":["(c)"],"by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bittner v. United States","cite":"143 S. Ct. 713","y":2023,"id":"2022/21-1195","sc":1,"by":{"2":"18–19"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"To the extent doubt persists at this point about the best reading of [§ 924(j)], a venerable principle supplies a way to resolve it","cite":"143 S. Ct. 713, 724"},{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"protect[s] the Due Process Clause’s promise that ‘a fair warning should be given to the world in language that the common world will understand, of what the law intends to do if a certain line is passed.’","cite":"143 S. Ct. at 725"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Borden v. United States","cite":"141 S. Ct. 1817","y":2021,"id":"2020/19-5410","sc":1,"by":{"2":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Commissioner v. Acker","cite":"361 U.S. 87","y":1959,"id":"1959/13","sc":1,"by":{"2":"18"},"q":[{"src":"brief","b":2,"text":"‘is not to be subjected to a penalty unless the words of the statute plainly impose it.’","cite":"361 U.S. 87, 91"}]},{"t":"case","label":"Hill v. U.S. ex rel. Wampler","cite":"298 U.S. 460","y":1936,"id":"scdb/1935-126","sc":1,"by":{"2":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"McBoyle v. United States","cite":"283 U.S. 25","y":1931,"id":"scdb/1930-139","sc":1,"by":{"2":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Whitman v. Am. Trucking Ass’ns","cite":"531 U.S. 457","y":2001,"id":"2000/99-1257","sc":1,"by":{"2":"8"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 249","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/249","by":{"2":"16"}},{"t":"usc","label":"21 U.S.C. § 960","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/21/960","by":{"2":"16"}},{"t":"usc","label":"33 U.S.C. § 1311","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/33/1311","by":{"2":"4–5"},"q":[{"src":"argument","who":"Mr. Rosenberg","text":"There, it was Section 1311. And it's the same issue here. Just because subsection (c) mentions -- excuse me, subsection (j) mentions subsection (c), it doesn't mean that the authority for the sentence comes out of subsection (c)."}]}]};